--TEST--
An `inout` argument names storage the callee can write back through, so an array element, a static property, a call's result and a literal are each refused at the argument — `rule:types/arrays`, `rule:php-migration/an-element-write-needs-storage-to-write-back-into`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The callee writes back through the reference after it returns, so an
// `inout` argument has to name storage that is still there to be written.
// This sweeps one spelling per shape that is not, in source order, so a shape
// that stopped refusing shows up as a missing line rather than as a write
// that lands somewhere nobody reads.
//
// The array element is the interesting one, and it is first: it looks like a
// place and is not. `rule:types/arrays`'s copy-on-write separation means the
// element the callee would be handed is a copy's, so the write-back would be
// invisible to the holder — the fix the help names is to read it into a
// local, pass that, and store it back where the copy is visible.
class P {
    public static int $count = 0;
    public int $n = 0;
    public static function bump(inout int $x): void { $x = $x + 1; }
    public static function make(): int { return 1; }
}

array<int> $a = [1];
P::bump(inout $a["0"]);
P::bump(inout P::$count);
P::bump(inout P::make());
P::bump(inout 7);

// The two shapes that are accepted, deliberately last: a bare local and a
// property of one. `nvs-ir` stages a reference from exactly these, so a line
// that started refusing either would leave that staging with nothing to do.
int $n = 1;
P $p = new P();
P::bump(inout $n);
P::bump(inout $p->n);
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0439]: an array element cannot be passed to an `inout` parameter
%A
error[E0439]: only a variable or a property can be passed to an `inout` parameter
%A
error[E0439]: only a variable or a property can be passed to an `inout` parameter
%A
error[E0439]: only a variable or a property can be passed to an `inout` parameter
%A
error: aborting due to 4 errors
